1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,440
So what's been the biggest culture shock of moving to france.
那么，搬到法国最大的文化冲击是什么？

2
00:00:03,440 --> 00:00:09,920
I think the biggest has been having to get all of my grocery items from different places.
我觉得最大的冲击，是所有日用食品都得从不同地方买。

3
00:00:09,920 --> 00:00:18,159
There's not like a main store like target or trader joe's for us folk um where you can get everything you need in one place.
不像美国人熟悉的 Target 或 Trader Joe's 那样，有一家主要商店能一次买齐需要的东西。

4
00:00:18,159 --> 00:00:20,720
Just depending on like the quality of the items you want.
嗯，还要看你想买的东西需要什么品质。

5
00:00:20,720 --> 00:00:25,509
And then also customer service that's been a big one that still to this day shocks me.
另外还有顾客服务，这是一大冲击，直到今天仍让我惊讶。

6
00:00:25,519 --> 00:00:30,560
Oh okay cuz that's something I think is a stereotype is that customer service is just not the same.
哦，好，因为我觉得有种刻板印象，就是这里的顾客服务确实不一样。

7
00:00:30,560 --> 00:00:33,030
Why do you think there is this difference.
你觉得为什么会有这种差异？

8
00:00:33,040 --> 00:00:38,720
So in the us you definitely work for tips so I think people are generally more cheery.
她认为在美国工作要靠小费，所以人们通常显得更热情愉快。

9
00:00:38,720 --> 00:00:45,040
But also know that the way that they treat you will kind of depend on what they get at the end of their service.
但他们也知道，自己对待你的方式，会影响服务结束时能得到多少。

10
00:00:45,040 --> 00:00:52,870
Um and it's also part of your work so if somebody sees you like if your boss sees you not very kind with someone your job could be on the line.
嗯，这也是工作的一部分，所以如果有人，比如老板，看见你对别人不太友善，你的工作可能就保不住了。

11
00:00:52,880 --> 00:00:58,480
And it's a lot easier to lose your job in the us here they get paid no matter what.
她还认为，在美国失去工作容易得多；而在这里，无论如何他们都能领到工资。

12
00:00:58,480 --> 00:01:03,359
And I think french people in general are a little bit more frank and honest.
她觉得法国人总体上更直接、坦率一些。

13
00:01:03,359 --> 00:01:10,720
They don't have this kind of like polite exterior that's not bs but like kindness.
她觉得他们没有那种礼貌的外在表现——她不是说那是胡扯，而是类似友善的表现。

14
00:01:10,720 --> 00:01:16,320
Um so they are just more real and sometimes they don't have a good day and that can come out on you.
嗯，所以她觉得他们更真实；有时候他们心情不好，情绪就可能表现在对你的态度上。

15
00:01:16,320 --> 00:01:22,320
I think that is nice though because I think sometimes you go to north america cuz I'm canadian and people will be really nice.
不过我觉得这也不错，因为有时候你去北美——我是加拿大人——人们会非常友善。

16
00:01:22,320 --> 00:01:26,400
But you don't know if that's a niceness because it's just forced politeness by society.
但你不知道这种友善，是不是只是社会要求下勉强维持的礼貌。

17
00:01:26,400 --> 00:01:30,310
Because when french people are nice to you it's a genuine connection that you're having very true.
因为她觉得，法国人对你友善时，你们建立的是真诚的联系。另一位回应：确实如此。

18
00:01:30,320 --> 00:01:35,040
I think a lot of people say that french people romanticize life they also complain a lot.
我觉得很多人说法国人会把生活浪漫化；他们也常常抱怨。

19
00:01:35,040 --> 00:01:40,080
But do you think that there's some truth to the romanticism of just everyday living the small things.
但你觉得，人们说日常生活、细微小事也有浪漫之处，这有没有几分道理？

20
00:01:40,080 --> 00:01:44,080
I think it's like the viv like people really take their time.
我觉得就像……她接着说，人们真的会慢慢来；前面 the viv 一词字幕识别不清。

21
00:01:44,080 --> 00:01:51,359
Um even in the corporate world when I was working in corporate people take breaks to go have a coffee and catch up with a coworker.
嗯，即使在公司里，她以前做公司职员时，人们也会休息一下，喝杯咖啡，和同事聊聊近况。

22
00:01:51,359 --> 00:01:56,880
Or take 2 hour lunches and you know if it is sunny they'll go out and sit outside.
或者午饭吃上两个小时；而且，如果有太阳，他们就会出去坐坐。

23
00:01:56,880 --> 00:02:04,720
If you go to any park on a sunny day you'll see people laying out and just like absorbing the sun or reading and enjoying the moment.
晴天去公园，会看到人们躺着晒太阳，或者读书、享受当下。

24
00:02:04,720 --> 00:02:11,520
And like really celebrating that you're allowed to slow down and just like enjoy each moment for what it is.
也像是在认真珍惜一件事：你可以慢下来，就按每个当下本来的样子去享受它。

25
00:02:11,520 --> 00:02:18,240
And that's something in the us that we don't see often because it's a very go go go culture so I appreciate that.
她说，这在美国并不常见，因为那里的文化总是忙个不停，所以她很欣赏这一点。

26
00:02:18,240 --> 00:02:23,590
That is a bit of a cliche that france is a nation of complainers do you think there's any truth to that.
有一种老套说法，说法国是个爱抱怨的民族。你觉得这种说法有道理吗？

27
00:02:23,600 --> 00:02:27,760
Oh yeah 100 like no doubt there is truth to that.
哦，有，百分之百，毫无疑问是有道理的。

28
00:02:27,760 --> 00:02:37,839
Um I found myself complaining more because I live here and I try and remind myself that I think the complaining comes from a place of knowing what's good.
嗯，我发现自己住在这里以后也更爱抱怨了；我会提醒自己，我觉得这种抱怨来自知道什么才是好的。

29
00:02:37,839 --> 00:02:43,189
I think french people really know what's good and what exists to be fair and just and right.
她觉得法国人确实知道什么是好的，以及怎样才算公平、公正、正确。

30
00:02:43,199 --> 00:02:48,320
And so when it's not they're very quick to complain about it and like stand up for what they think is right.
所以事情不符合这些标准时，他们很快就会抱怨，维护自己认为正确的事情。

31
00:02:48,320 --> 00:02:53,200
Um and that that cliche kind of manifests into the protests that we have.
嗯，而这种老套印象，也体现在他们这里的抗议活动中。

32
00:02:53,200 --> 00:03:00,710
I think french people are notorious protesters like anything that they don't find just or right they will stand against it.
她觉得法国人以抗议闻名；凡是他们觉得不公平、不正确的事情，他们都会反对。

33
00:03:00,720 --> 00:03:07,200
People think french people like have a lot of arguments but they're more discussions and it's an open space.
人们以为法国人经常吵架，但她觉得那更多是讨论，是一个开放的交流空间。

34
00:03:07,200 --> 00:03:12,630
To really discuss what you believe without it being anything against the person or what they believe.
可以认真谈论自己的信念，而不意味着针对那个人，或针对对方的信念。

35
00:03:12,640 --> 00:03:17,679
But that has also led to amazing workers rights and things like that so it has led to a lot of positive things.
但她也认为，这带来了很好的劳动者权益之类的成果，所以确实产生了许多积极影响。

36
00:03:17,679 --> 00:03:25,030
Yeah I think when you see what it's led to you understand that it's something to admire rather than like complain about.
是的，我觉得看到它带来的结果，就会理解这是值得欣赏的事情，而不是值得抱怨的事情。
